('127.476.d.10.E',3,'To make 1 liter of 1 molar solution of a compound, you dissolve one:','["mole of the compound", "gram of the compound", "liter of the compound", "cup of the compound"]'::jsonb,0,'Molarity is moles per liter.','original'),
('127.476.d.10.E',4,'A solution is made with 0.5 mole of solute in 2 liters. Its molarity is:','["4 M", "0.25 M", "1 M", "0.5 M"]'::jsonb,1,'0.5 / 2 = 0.25.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.A',3,'The path that a biotechnology product follows from the early idea through testing to market is the product:','["closet", "gallery", "pipeline", "parade"]'::jsonb,2,'The pipeline moves ideas to products.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.A',4,'Stages in a typical drug pipeline include discovery, preclinical testing, clinical trials, and:','["auctions", "fashion shows", "decoration", "approval"]'::jsonb,3,'Approval follows trials.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.B',3,'Checking that a product meets standards before it is sold is part of quality:','["control", "comedy", "fashion", "tourism"]'::jsonb,0,'QC tests products.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.B',4,'Quality assurance focuses on preventing problems by managing the whole:','["holiday", "process", "menu", "playlist"]'::jsonb,1,'QA is process oriented.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.C',3,'Records showing how each batch was made are important because they let a company trace and fix:','["fashions", "birthdays", "problems", "parking"]'::jsonb,2,'Documentation enables traceability.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.C',4,'If it was not written down, in a regulated lab it is treated as if it did not happen, which shows the importance of:','["volume", "decoration", "speed", "documentation"]'::jsonb,3,'Documentation is evidence.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.D',3,'After an error is found, a company fixes it and takes steps to prevent it from happening again. This is called corrective and preventive:','["action", "fashion", "dining", "music"]'::jsonb,0,'CAPA fixes and prevents.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.D',4,'CAPA is used when an audit finds that a step was skipped. The company would investigate the root cause and then:','["skip the step again", "change the process", "delete the audit", "blame a visitor"]'::jsonb,1,'CAPA improves the process.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.E',3,'A Quality Management System includes inspections, audits, and written:','["jokes", "menus", "procedures", "games"]'::jsonb,2,'QMS has procedures and audits.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.E',4,'An audit in a QMS checks whether the company is following its own:','["vacations", "hobbies", "fashions", "procedures"]'::jsonb,3,'Audits verify compliance.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.F',3,'Good Manufacturing Practices are rules that make sure products are consistently made and controlled to quality:','["standards", "rumors", "moods", "trends"]'::jsonb,0,'GMP ensures quality.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.F',4,'Good Clinical Practices protect the rights and safety of people who take part in:','["sports games", "clinical trials", "parades", "races"]'::jsonb,1,'GCP is for human research.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.G',3,'Checking that an instrument gives accurate and repeatable results is called:','["shipping", "packaging", "validation", "decoration"]'::jsonb,2,'Validation proves performance.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.G',4,'Running a known standard on an instrument and recording the result is a part of equipment:','["tourism", "packing", "advertising", "validation testing"]'::jsonb,3,'Standards verify instruments.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.H',3,'A technician compares the results of a validation test with acceptance limits using:','["statistics", "rumors", "guessing", "coin flips"]'::jsonb,0,'Statistics evaluate performance.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.H',4,'If the percent error is outside the limit, the equipment fails and the technician must:','["hide the data", "report and correct the problem", "change the limit", "ignore the result"]'::jsonb,1,'Failures are reported.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.I',3,'A laboratory method protocol gives step-by-step instructions so that anyone trained can perform the test the:','["wrong way", "quick way", "same way", "secret way"]'::jsonb,2,'Protocols standardize work.','original'),
('127.476.d.11.I',4,'A scientist writing a new protocol should include materials, safety notes, steps, and the expected:','["fashions", "rumors", "pets", "results"]'::jsonb,3,'Protocols describe expected results.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.A',3,'In a medical chart, the abbreviation BP stands for:','["blood pressure", "body posture", "breathing pace", "bone problem"]'::jsonb,0,'BP is a standard abbreviation for blood pressure.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.A',4,'The abbreviation NPO on a patient''s chart means the patient should take:','["food at noon", "nothing by mouth", "a double dose", "extra water"]'::jsonb,1,'NPO comes from Latin for nothing by mouth.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.B',3,'Writing a trailing zero, such as 5.0, is on the do-not-use list because a missed decimal point could make it be read as:','["0.5", "0.05", "50", "5.5"]'::jsonb,2,'Trailing zeros can cause tenfold errors.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.B',4,'In a medication order, the letter U can be mistaken for the number 0 or 4, so safer practice is to write out the word:','["use", "ulcer", "upper", "units"]'::jsonb,3,'Spelling out units avoids errors.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.C',3,'In the word gastritis, the part gastr means stomach and the ending itis means:','["inflammation", "study", "pain relief", "removal"]'::jsonb,0,'-itis means inflammation.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.C',4,'In the word nephrectomy, the root nephr means kidney, and the suffix ectomy means:','["study of", "surgical removal", "inflammation", "condition of"]'::jsonb,1,'-ectomy is surgical removal.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.D',3,'Using the root cardi for heart and the suffix logy for study of, the word that means the study of the heart is:','["cardiectomy", "cardiomegaly", "cardiology", "cardiopathy"]'::jsonb,2,'cardi + logy.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.D',4,'Building a word from the root derm for skin and the suffix itis gives the term for skin inflammation:','["dermalgia", "dermectomy", "dermatology", "dermatitis"]'::jsonb,3,'derm + atitis.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.E',3,'An eponym is a medical term named after a person, such as:','["Alzheimer''s disease", "blood pressure", "body weight", "heart rate"]'::jsonb,0,'Alzheimer''s is named for Alois Alzheimer.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.E',4,'A movement disorder is named for the English physician who described it in 1817. That physician was James:','["Fleming", "Parkinson", "Salk", "Pasteur"]'::jsonb,1,'Eponyms honor discoverers.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.F',3,'The directional term meaning toward the front of the body is:','["superior", "lateral", "anterior", "posterior"]'::jsonb,2,'Anterior is front.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.F',4,'The plane that divides the body into right and left halves is the:','["coronal plane", "transverse plane", "oblique plane", "sagittal plane"]'::jsonb,3,'Sagittal divides left and right.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.G',3,'The term hypertension is spelled with the prefix hyper, meaning above or too much, and refers to high:','["blood pressure", "sugar", "body weight", "iron"]'::jsonb,0,'Hypertension is high blood pressure.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.G',4,'Correct spelling matters in health care because a one-letter difference, such as ileum and ilium, can change the:','["paper", "meaning", "font", "printer"]'::jsonb,1,'Ileum is bowel, ilium is hip bone.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.H',3,'A student who knows that brady means slow and cardia refers to the heart can use prior knowledge to figure out that bradycardia is a:','["heart attack", "heart murmur", "slow heart rate", "fast heart rate"]'::jsonb,2,'brady = slow.','original'),
('127.477.d.1.H',4,'Using the prefix poly meaning many and the root uria meaning urine, polyuria means:','["painful urination", "no urine", "blood in urine", "excessive urination"]'::jsonb,3,'poly + uria.','original'),
('127.477.d.2.A',3,'When speaking to a patient or team, a health worker should pronounce terms:','["clearly and correctly", "in slang", "as fast as possible", "quietly"]'::jsonb,0,'Clear speech prevents errors.','original'),
('127.477.d.2.A',4,'When giving a report, the nurse says the numbers and names slowly, then asks the listener to repeat them back to confirm:','["credit", "understanding", "schedule", "payment"]'::jsonb,1,'Repeat-back confirms accuracy.','original'),
('127.477.d.2.B',3,'Instead of saying the patient hurts, a nurse might say the patient has sharp pain in the right lower quadrant. This is more:','["vague", "funny", "precise", "artistic"]'::jsonb,2,'Precise language aids communication.','original'),
('127.477.d.2.B',4,'Describing a wound as 3 centimeters long and red with drainage is better than saying it looks bad because it is more:','["poetic", "slow", "colorful", "specific"]'::jsonb,3,'Specifics are measurable.','original'),
('127.477.d.2.C',3,'A health worker explains the term hypotension to a patient by saying it means:','["low blood pressure", "fast breathing", "a broken bone", "high fever"]'::jsonb,0,'Translate into plain language.','original'),
('127.477.d.2.C',4,'Translating the term myocardial infarction into words a patient understands, we would say:','["a cold", "a heart attack", "a sprained ankle", "a skin rash"]'::jsonb,1,'Plain language aids understanding.','original'),
('127.477.d.3.A',3,'A student who is not sure what the term arrhythmia means could look in a:','["cookbook", "poem book", "medical dictionary", "atlas"]'::jsonb,2,'Dictionaries define terms.','original'),
('127.477.d.3.A',4,'Medical dictionaries often list a word''s root, definition, and:','["route", "prize", "recipe", "pronunciation"]'::jsonb,3,'Entries guide pronunciation.','original'),
('127.477.d.3.B',3,'To interpret a lab report, a student might use a dictionary along with a table of:','["normal values", "comics", "games", "tickets"]'::jsonb,0,'Reference tables explain results.','original'),
('127.477.d.3.B',4,'Combining a medical dictionary, an anatomy diagram, and a lab table to understand a report is an example of integrating:','["holidays", "resources", "hobbies", "rumors"]'::jsonb,1,'Several resources work together.','original'),
('127.477.d.3.C',3,'When looking up medical terms online, a student should use reliable sites and be supervised by a:','["stranger", "random chat", "teacher or supervisor", "game friend"]'::jsonb,2,'Supervision keeps research safe.','original'),
('127.477.d.3.C',4,'Websites of government health agencies and universities are good for medical research because they are:','["fiction", "unreviewed", "anonymous", "credible"]'::jsonb,3,'Credibility matters.','original'),
('127.477.d.4.A',3,'The abbreviation q.i.d. means four times a day, while b.i.d. means:','["twice a day", "at bedtime", "every hour", "once a day"]'::jsonb,0,'b.i.d. is twice daily.','original'),
('127.477.d.4.A',4,'Abbreviations like t.i.d. and q.i.d. are used in prescriptions to show how often to take a medicine, called the:','["brand", "frequency", "cost", "color"]'::jsonb,1,'They indicate frequency.','original'),
('127.477.d.4.B',3,'In a simulated medical record, the note Pt c/o SOB means the patient complains of:','["stomach ache", "sore bones", "shortness of breath", "skin burn"]'::jsonb,2,'SOB is shortness of breath.','original'),
('127.477.d.4.B',4,'Translating the order give 2 tabs po q.i.d. in a simulated chart, the po part means the medicine is taken:','["by injection", "in the eye", "on the skin", "by mouth"]'::jsonb,3,'po = per os.','original'),
('127.477.d.5.A',3,'A transcriptionist listens to a doctor''s recording and types the notes. This job requires knowing medical:','["vocabulary", "cooking", "sports", "music"]'::jsonb,0,'Transcription needs vocabulary.','original'),
('127.477.d.5.A',4,'When interpreting a dictated report, the transcriptionist must tell apart words that sound alike, such as hypo and hyper, so that the report is:','["short", "accurate", "loud", "slow"]'::jsonb,1,'Soundalike terms cause errors.','original'),
('127.477.d.5.B',3,'To help a patient understand, a nurse says sugar level for the term:','["calcium", "oxygen", "glucose", "pressure"]'::jsonb,2,'Plain translation of glucose.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
insert into public.question_bank (teks_code, teks_item_id, subject, grade_num, course, difficulty, qtype, prompt, choices, answer_idx, explanation, source, status, gen_batch)
select distinct on (v.prompt) v.code, i.id, d.subject,
       case when i.grade_level = 'K' then 0 when i.grade_level ~ '^[0-9]+' then substring(i.grade_level from '^[0-9]+')::smallint end,
       case when i.grade_level is null then i.grade_course_title end,
       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_70_v1'
from (values
('127.477.d.5.B',4,'A nurse turns the phrase the patient is febrile into conversational language by saying the patient has a:','["limp", "cough", "rash", "fever"]'::jsonb,3,'Febrile means fever.','original'),
('127.477.d.5.C',3,'A doctor who specializes in the heart is a cardiologist, and one who treats skin conditions is a:','["dermatologist", "neurologist", "oncologist", "urologist"]'::jsonb,0,'Dermatology is skin.','original'),
('127.477.d.5.C',4,'A physician who specializes in treating cancer is called an:','["pediatrician", "oncologist", "ophthalmologist", "podiatrist"]'::jsonb,1,'Oncology treats cancer.','original'),
('127.477.d.5.D',3,'A nurse writes that the patient has a red, swollen, painful knee. She is summarizing her observations using:','["art", "jokes", "medical terminology", "slang"]'::jsonb,2,'Terminology summarizes observations.','original'),
('127.477.d.5.D',4,'Summarizing findings as erythema and edema of the knee uses medical words for redness and:','["hunger", "weight loss", "pain relief", "swelling"]'::jsonb,3,'Edema is swelling.','original'),
('127.477.d.5.E',3,'A scenario says the patient has dyspnea and tachycardia. Correctly interpreting this means the patient has difficulty breathing and a:','["fast heart rate", "low fever", "normal pulse", "slow heart rate"]'::jsonb,0,'Tachy means fast.','original'),
('127.477.d.5.E',4,'In a medical scenario, a patient has hematuria. Correct interpretation is that the patient has:','["blood in the stool", "blood in the urine", "low blood sugar", "a sore throat"]'::jsonb,1,'Hemat + uria.','original'),
('127.478.d.1.A',3,'A patient is scared before a procedure. A caring health worker responds with:','["silence and a frown", "a joke at the patient''s expense", "empathy and a calm voice", "impatience"]'::jsonb,2,'Compassion builds trust.','original'),
('127.478.d.1.A',4,'Empathy means trying to understand how a patient feels, while compassion adds a desire to:','["judge", "argue", "leave", "help"]'::jsonb,3,'Compassion motivates action.','original'),
('127.478.d.1.B',3,'When explaining a diagnosis to a patient who does not know medical terms, a health worker should use:','["plain, clear language", "technical jargon", "whispers", "long words"]'::jsonb,0,'Plain language helps understanding.','original'),
('127.478.d.1.B',4,'Communicating accurately and efficiently means giving information that is correct, complete, and:','["rushed", "easy to understand", "hidden", "vague"]'::jsonb,1,'Clarity matters.','original'),
('127.478.d.1.C',3,'A federal law that protects the privacy of patient health information is:','["FERPA", "OSHA", "HIPAA", "ADA"]'::jsonb,2,'HIPAA covers health privacy.','original'),
('127.478.d.1.C',4,'Discussing a patient''s condition in a hallway where others can hear would most likely violate:','["a dress code", "a fire code", "a parking policy", "patient privacy rules"]'::jsonb,3,'Privacy rules require discretion.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.A',3,'A nurse needs to give 2 tablets that are each 250 mg. The total dose is:','["500 mg", "1,000 mg", "252 mg", "125 mg"]'::jsonb,0,'2 x 250 = 500.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.A',4,'A patient receives 750 mL of IV fluid over 5 hours. The hourly rate is:','["15 mL", "150 mL", "3,750 mL", "375 mL"]'::jsonb,1,'750 / 5 = 150.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.B',3,'When charting, health workers write facts that are objective, accurate, and:','["secret", "decorative", "timely", "emotional"]'::jsonb,2,'Documentation should be timely.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.B',4,'If an error is made in a paper chart, the worker should draw a single line through it, write error, and add initials, instead of using:','["a signature", "a pen", "a date", "correction fluid"]'::jsonb,3,'Never obscure the original entry.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.C',3,'A patient information sheet about a new medicine includes words the nurse does not know. The nurse should:','["look up the terms", "skip the sheet", "guess", "hand it over unread"]'::jsonb,0,'Technical material requires careful reading.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.C',4,'When reading a complex research article on treatment, a worker pays close attention to the methods and the:','["ads", "results", "page numbers", "font"]'::jsonb,1,'Findings matter.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.D',3,'The body keeps its internal conditions stable, such as temperature and blood sugar, through a process called:','["mutation", "hibernation", "homeostasis", "digestion"]'::jsonb,2,'Homeostasis is balance.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.D',4,'When you get too warm, your body sweats to cool down, an example of a feedback mechanism that maintains:','["inflammation", "blood loss", "infection", "homeostasis"]'::jsonb,3,'Negative feedback restores balance.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.E',3,'Diseases such as influenza spread across countries, which shows the global impact of:','["infectious disease", "sports", "fashion", "weather only"]'::jsonb,0,'Disease crosses borders.','original'),
('127.478.d.2.E',4,'Researching how vaccines affect global health helps health workers understand how prevention can lower the cost of:','["furniture", "treatment", "music", "travel"]'::jsonb,1,'Prevention reduces costs.','original'),
('127.478.d.3.A',3,'Therapeutic communication is a way of speaking and listening that supports the patient''s:','["schedule", "boredom", "well-being", "payment"]'::jsonb,2,'Therapeutic communication helps healing.','original'),
('127.478.d.3.A',4,'A nurse says, I can see this is hard for you. Tell me more. This is an example of:','["lecturing", "blaming", "avoidance", "active listening"]'::jsonb,3,'Open-ended listening supports patients.','original'),
('127.478.d.3.B',3,'Facing a patient, making eye contact, and nodding are examples of:','["non-verbal communication", "telephone communication", "sign errors", "written communication"]'::jsonb,0,'Body language communicates.','original'),
('127.478.d.3.B',4,'A patient who is hard of hearing may need the worker to face them, speak clearly, and use:','["louder yelling only", "gestures or written notes", "turning away", "whispering"]'::jsonb,1,'Adapt communication to the person.','original'),
('127.478.d.3.C',3,'Using a smartphone for personal texts while caring for a patient is:','["normal", "required", "inappropriate", "helpful"]'::jsonb,2,'Devices should not distract from care.','original'),
('127.478.d.3.C',4,'In a clinical setting, taking a photo of a patient with a personal phone could break patient:','["billing", "parking", "scheduling", "confidentiality"]'::jsonb,3,'Photos can violate privacy.','original'),
('127.478.d.4.A',3,'Having supportive relationships at work helps people perform better because they feel:','["valued and supported", "ignored", "isolated", "bored"]'::jsonb,0,'Healthy relationships boost performance.','original'),
('127.478.d.4.A',4,'A student with a trusting relationship with her supervisor is more likely to ask questions and learn, which improves her:','["absences", "performance", "delays", "complaints"]'::jsonb,1,'Trust encourages learning.','original'),
('127.478.d.4.B',3,'Skills such as listening, showing respect, and being honest help build and maintain healthy:','["debts", "traffic", "relationships", "weather"]'::jsonb,2,'Communication sustains relationships.','original'),
('127.478.d.4.B',4,'Using I statements like I feel worried when plans change helps a person communicate without:','["listening", "feeling", "thinking", "blaming"]'::jsonb,3,'I statements avoid blame.','original'),
('127.478.d.4.C',3,'Telling a coworker calmly what you need is a way of communicating your:','["needs", "secrets", "wishes for others only", "debts"]'::jsonb,0,'Express needs directly.','original'),
('127.478.d.4.C',4,'When feeling frustrated, a healthy strategy is to take a breath and describe the feeling in words instead of:','["walking", "lashing out", "writing", "pausing"]'::jsonb,1,'Regulate emotions.','original'),
('127.478.d.4.D',3,'Compromise, active listening, and finding a middle ground are ways to resolve a:','["holiday", "schedule", "conflict", "bill"]'::jsonb,2,'Conflict resolution tools.','original'),
('127.478.d.4.D',4,'To judge whether a conflict-resolution technique worked, a team should check whether the problem was solved and the relationship was:','["ended", "ignored", "delayed", "preserved"]'::jsonb,3,'Effective resolution fixes the issue and keeps trust.','original'),
('127.478.d.5.A',3,'A patient''s chart, a dental record, and a lab report are examples of health document:','["formats", "menus", "rumors", "games"]'::jsonb,0,'Records have set formats.','original'),
('127.478.d.5.A',4,'Dental and medical records have different forms, but both must be kept accurate and:','["public", "confidential", "decorative", "loud"]'::jsonb,1,'Both are protected.','original'),
('127.478.d.5.B',3,'Industry-based standards for health documents help make sure that records are consistent and:','["funny", "artistic", "legal", "casual"]'::jsonb,2,'Standards ensure legal validity.','original'),
('127.478.d.5.B',4,'Using the SOAP format (subjective, objective, assessment, plan) to prepare a note follows a documentation:','["hobby", "joke", "menu", "standard"]'::jsonb,3,'SOAP is a standard note format.','original'),
('127.478.d.5.C',3,'Recording patient information in an electronic health record helps providers share data quickly. The system is often shortened to:','["EHR", "ATM", "USB", "GPS"]'::jsonb,0,'EHR means electronic health record.','original'),
('127.478.d.5.C',4,'When using an electronic record, you should log out when finished so that others cannot see the patient''s:','["lunch", "information", "calendar", "keys"]'::jsonb,1,'Logging out protects privacy.','original'),
('127.478.d.6.A',3,'To work as a registered nurse, a person must graduate from an approved program and pass a licensing exam. This is called:','["trading", "banking", "licensure", "tourism"]'::jsonb,2,'Licensure is legal permission to practice.','original'),
('127.478.d.6.A',4,'Many health professionals must complete continuing education to keep their licenses or certifications current. This helps them:','["lose skills", "avoid work", "skip tests", "stay up to date"]'::jsonb,3,'CE keeps knowledge current.','original'),
('127.478.d.6.B',3,'When applying for a health science job, a candidate may submit a resume and attend an:','["interview", "audition", "parade", "concert"]'::jsonb,0,'Interviews follow applications.','original'),
('127.478.d.6.B',4,'After a job interview, a polite step that shows professionalism is to send a:','["demand", "thank-you note", "complaint", "joke"]'::jsonb,1,'Follow-up is courteous.','original'),
('127.478.d.7.A',3,'A health care team solves a problem by analyzing the situation, brainstorming options, and reaching:','["chaos", "separation", "consensus", "silence"]'::jsonb,2,'Consensus-building is a teamwork skill.','original'),
('127.478.d.7.A',4,'Critical thinking in health care means weighing evidence before acting and being willing to adapt when conditions:','["freeze", "repeat", "improve only", "change"]'::jsonb,3,'Adaptability is key.','original'),
('127.478.d.7.B',3,'Choosing a treatment affects patient health, cost, and the work of staff. Evaluating these effects is evaluating the decision''s:','["impact", "font", "color", "popularity"]'::jsonb,0,'Decisions have impacts.','original'),
('127.478.d.7.B',4,'A hospital shifts to electronic records. Staff can judge the impact of that decision by checking for fewer:','["patients", "errors", "keys", "windows"]'::jsonb,1,'Outcomes reveal impact.','original'),
('127.478.d.7.C',3,'If a plan is not improving patient outcomes, the team should suggest:','["blame", "excuses", "modifications", "delay"]'::jsonb,2,'Plans should be adjusted.','original'),
('127.478.d.7.C',4,'After noticing that patients are waiting too long, a team suggests changing the check-in process. This is modifying a plan based on:','["accidents", "feelings only", "luck", "outcomes"]'::jsonb,3,'Use outcomes to improve.','original'),
('127.478.d.8.A',3,'Before entering a room with a patient who has a contagious disease, health workers follow safety standards such as wearing:','["protective equipment", "jewelry", "scarves", "headphones"]'::jsonb,0,'PPE reduces transmission.','original'),
('127.478.d.8.A',4,'A sharps container is used for needles to comply with safety requirements and prevent:','["paper cuts only", "needle-stick injuries", "bad weather", "fires"]'::jsonb,1,'Sharps containers protect workers.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
